  Product key:  CU-PERFDEF-022
  Release:      June 3, 2026 (v2.6)

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Purpose:
    Defers specified third-party scripts past window.load, firing them
    when the main thread is idle and no recent user input. Reduces Total
    Blocking Time (TBT) without removing functionality.

  Mechanism:
    Scripts — intercepted via HTMLScriptElement.prototype.src patching,
             Element.prototype.setAttribute patching, and a MutationObserver.
             Original script elements are NEUTRALIZED (type swapped, src
             cleared) but kept in DOM so id-based references resolve.
             When fired, a new script loads with original data-* attributes
             preserved, then a synthetic load event is dispatched on the
             original element so listeners still fire.

  Safety:
    - Long-task + input idleness check before firing
    - Back-off retry (up to 10 attempts with increasing delay)
    - Hard fallback fires all remaining after max attempts
    - MutationObserver auto-disconnects after 30s
    - Re-fire recursion prevented via data-chaos-refired marker
    - data-* attributes cloned from original to re-fired element
      (preserves config for scripts reading document.currentScript.dataset)

  Debug:
    Set DEBUG = true only during development. Console logs are prefixed
    [deferrer-v2] for filtering.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  v2.6 CHANGELOG (June 3, 2026):
    Removed 'klaviyo' from Handlers_Deferred_Scripts.
    Root cause: double-deferral race with chaos_unleashed_cfh_defer.liquid
    Pass 2 (v1.2+). CFH defer neutralizes the Klaviyo bootstrap at
    server-render time (type="text/x-cu-deferred") and restores it on
    scroll/touchstart. This Performance Deferrer's prototype.src patch
    was re-intercepting the CFH restorer's re-injected <script>, creating
    a double-queue scenario. Observed effect: Klaviyo popup was not
    rendering in most sessions — the bootstrap was stuck in the 10-attempt
    backoff loop, delaying load 5-15s or until hard-fallback.
    CFH defer alone is the correct single layer for Klaviyo.
    Do not re-add 'klaviyo' to this list.

  v2.5 CHANGELOG (April 25, 2026):
    Added 'klaviyo' to Handlers_Deferred_Scripts. Catches klaviyo.js
    bootstrap (parser-discovered, potentially_blocking) plus the
    commit-{hash} runtime bundles that load Klaviyo's popup/form/event
    machinery. Substring 'klaviyo' matches both static.klaviyo.com and
    a.klaviyo.com URLs, future-proof against subdomain or hash rotation.
    Trade-off: Klaviyo popups fire ~1-3 seconds later in real-world
    conditions. Most popups are configured with a 5-30 second display
    delay timer anyway, so net user-visible delay is typically zero.
    Email capture functionality unchanged for users who stay on the
    page beyond ~3 seconds (which is essentially everyone who didn't
    immediately bounce).

  v2.4 CHANGELOG (April 25, 2026):
    Added '/wpm/' to Handlers_Deferred_Scripts. Catches Shopify's web
    pixels manager bundle (cdn/wpm/s{shop_hash}m.js) which was loading
    render-blocking at the LCP measurement window and competing with
    LCP-critical resources for slow-network bandwidth. Pattern uses
    leading slash to anchor the match to the URL path segment, avoiding
    false positives on words containing 'wpm' elsewhere. Trade-off:
    Customer Events (Klaviyo, Pinterest, TikTok, Facebook, Google Ads,
    AfterShip) fire after window.load instead of during initial render;
    page_view counts may under-report by 1-3% for fast-bounce traffic.
    Conversion attribution unaffected (conversion events fire much later
    in the user journey, well after wpm has fired multiple times).

  v2.3 CHANGELOG (April 24, 2026):
    CORRECTION: v2.0-2.2 advertised CSS deferral ("stylesheets intercepted
    same three paths, media forced to print, flipped back to all post-load").
    That path did not work as advertised. Chrome commits render-blocking
    priority for <link rel="stylesheet"> at HTML tokenizer time; the
    setAttribute patch and MutationObserver callback both run after the
    commitment. Flipping media to "print" via those hooks arrives too late
    to prevent the render-blocking fetch. Lighthouse consistently classified
    the targeted Judge.me carousels.css as render-blocking with wastedMs
    810ms across every benchmark run we captured.

    The script defer path IS functional. It stays.

    v2.3 prunes the placebo CSS path:
      - Overlay_Deferred_Css array now empty (signature variable preserved)
      - Apply_Css_One now a no-op returning false (signature variable preserved)
      - setAttribute patch no longer branches on 'href'/stylesheet links
      - MutationObserver no longer branches on LINK nodes
      - Hard_Fallback_Fire and fireWhenSafe drop CSS checks
      - deferredCss array removed (no longer referenced)

    All 22 signature variables retain their names and declaration order;
    the CHAOSUNLEASHED and LBPERF26 acrostics still verify.

    For Judge.me CSS deferral that actually moves Lighthouse, see the
    server-side approach: content_for_header rewrite or preload-ahead
    hints emitted from Liquid. That work lives outside this module.

